Implemented the Track B generator, but it is not yet shippable because the required oracle evidence could not run.

- [Generator](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1111.3996/gen_1111_3996.py)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1111.3996/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1111.3996/README.md)
- [Bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1111.3996/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1111.3996/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1111.3996/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

The family uses the paper’s Theorem 1 3SAT-to-overlapping-PAFP reduction, while avoiding the polynomial well-parenthesized and halving regimes identified in the [full paper](https://arxiv.org/html/1111.3996v3).

Local results:

- G1–G8 pass.
- Five attacks each fail 0/8.
- Gaussian elimination and DPLL succeed 8/8 and are honestly disclosed as Track B reference algorithms.
- Guessing: 0/200,000 from the structure-aware `2^251` space.
- Answer: 65 characters, 251 semantic atoms.
- Compact route: 108 operations.
- G9 remains failed because all bare, hinted, and placebo calls returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` before any scored attempt.

The error-only transcripts were retained for diagnosis, but they are not hardness evidence. Restoring OpenRouter quota and rerunning the three harness arms is the only remaining work.
